## {{ record.title }} · `sec:toolchain:record`

**Decision (Compiler)** · `dec:toolchain:compiler` The workspace pins {{ policy.channel }} with {{ policy.components | join(" and ") }} under (`amend:toolchain:dated-compiler`). The dated compiler and its required components remain installed on every compute node while the repository names it; newer dated installs coexist with it. Gates and fuzz campaigns use this one named compiler. Its expected rustc commit is {{ policy.rustc_commit }}, publicly located at https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/commit/{{ policy.rustc_commit }}. Move the pin only in its own gated commit, retaining the answering tool versions in the gate log.

**Decision (Minimum compiler)** · `dec:toolchain:minimum` The workspace and its first-party members declare no minimum compiler version. The gate checks one pinned compiler, so a minimum for older compilers would still be a claim nothing checks, and no downstream consumer builds this workspace on an older compiler.

**Decision (Release profile)** · `dec:toolchain:release` Releases use {{ policy.lto }} LTO and {{ policy.codegen_units }} codegen unit. Thin LTO provides most of full LTO's cross-crate inlining at a fraction of its link time, and one codegen unit lets the optimiser see each crate whole. Dev and test profiles retain Cargo defaults so gate builds keep their incremental speed; changing their optimisation levels requires later measurement.

**Gate (Verification)** · `gate:toolchain:verification` The {{ policy.toolchain_gate }} step requires exactly {{ policy.channel }}, both declared components, exact equality between rustc -vV's commit-hash and {{ policy.rustc_commit }}, and answering compiler, Cargo, Clippy and formatter commands, and logs rustc -vV, cargo -V, cargo clippy -V and rustfmt -V. The {{ policy.profile_gate }} step refuses minimum compiler declarations in the workspace and first-party members and requires the reviewed profiles under (`amend:toolchain:measured-gate-profile`). The {{ policy.tests_gate }} step witnesses acceptance of the pinned dated channel and rejection of the undated channel, another date, stable releases, a wrong or missing compiler commit-hash, and missing components or command responses. The compiler identity check refuses an overriding nightly or PATH-selected compiler whose answering commit differs even when the channel file is correct.

**Amendment (Development performance measurements)** · `amend:toolchain:development-performance` The current defaults and refusal of dev/test overrides remain the implemented policy until the foundation bite of (`gate:devperf:foundation`). That bite measures and decides the dev/test settings under (`dec:devperf:profiles`), then updates the profile declarations, checker and witnesses together to enforce the reviewed choices. This refines (`dec:toolchain:release`) and (`gate:toolchain:verification`) without changing the compiler policy or the release profile. Build-time excess is a warning and an owned misconfiguration finding, never a failing gate verdict (`rule:devperf:builds`), (`rule:devperf:nonverdict`).

**Amendment (Measured gate profile)** · `amend:toolchain:measured-gate-profile` This settles (`amend:toolchain:development-performance`) and refines (`dec:toolchain:release`) and (`gate:toolchain:verification`) through the measured choice in (`amend:devperf:measured-choices`). {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.measurements', name='foundation-decisions').profile }} The build-policy checker requires exactly the reviewed release and gate profiles and continues to refuse dev/test overrides. Its witnesses reject changed optimization or symbols and disabled debug assertions or overflow checks. Gate Clippy, test, documentation and document-engine commands select the same named profile; timing findings remain separate from their semantic status. A second optimized profile and its compile-time kind selection are implemented in the common-boundary cut: release-debug inherits release, retains its optimization, LTO, codegen units, assertion, overflow and panic settings, and adds debug information level two with no stripping. Its reviewed recipe also selects the production debug-observation feature; release uses the release profile without that feature. The feature fixes the kind at compile time, while the recipe binds kind to the effective profile and feature pair. A profile name or debug_assertions alone cannot identify the kind. Ordinary dev/test and the gate profile remain unchanged. The exact profile checker, build-policy dataset, feature classifications and their witnesses admit the third profile with the manifest. Separately compiled shipped recipe artifacts are registered and controlled at their package-scoped profile and feature selections; their warm gate-step cost is measured under (`rule:devperf:measurement`) without a speed verdict; a clean-build figure with empty target artifacts and compiler-cache misses remains owed. The existing protected-package selection separately admits the debug generator under (`amend:decomposition:protected-package-resolution`). Added gate-build cost is measured under (`rule:devperf:builds`) and (`rule:devperf:nonverdict`), not inferred from this design.

**Amendment (Dated compiler)** · `amend:toolchain:dated-compiler` This amends (`dec:toolchain:compiler`) and (`gate:toolchain:verification`): a retained dated install makes the compiler identity reproducible, while an undated channel can select a different compiler from the dated channel used by fuzz campaigns. Pinning the shared channel aligns those checks and makes a compiler move an explicit, gated change. The pin does not establish older-compiler compatibility or universal artifact reproducibility; (`dec:toolchain:minimum`) and the reviewed build profiles remain unchanged.

For the native numerical surface of (`rule:design:binary64-evidence`), the dependency's undated nightly channel is evidence of its build convention, not a workspace compiler selection. The compatibility study found that the dated compiler rejected a const BTreeMap length call in the candidate CAS source; its newer dated trial reached consumer errors, including new fault variants, observation-lifetime linting and changed test ownership, rather than a green workspace gate. That trial establishes neither a minimum compatible date nor qualification of the current capability baseline. Recommend a separately gated dated-compiler move only after a named candidate runs the complete workspace: formatting, every enabled lint, all package and package-alone protected tests, documentation/edge/hash checks and the full gate, retaining exact compiler/component identities and both failing and passing evidence. Re-review every pre-one dependency's used API, feature and build-time behavior under that compiler, including unchanged versions; a compiler update does not excuse a changed interface or lint by suppression. Compare the gate's configuration identity and resource/reference baselines under their existing rules; do not carry an incomparable measurement across the move or change a budget to obtain admission. The installed dated compiler that passes this witness, the source's compatibility with it, the whole-workspace repair delta and its measured gate costs remain held. An undated channel or an untried date is not selected here; (`dec:toolchain:compiler`) requires the move in its own gated commit, and the numerical dependency admission follows it.

**Amendment (Build composition)** · `amend:toolchain:build-composition` The composition layer of (`rec:buildgraph:decision`) delegates Rust work to Cargo with the pinned compiler, reviewed profiles and exact feature selections. It changes neither (`amend:toolchain:dated-compiler`) nor (`amend:toolchain:measured-gate-profile`). The graph records these configuration inputs and answering versions; a fresh graph run is not authority to change them or reuse stale test evidence.
